module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.unbound_generic_reaches_conformance_unjudged

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data unbound_generic_reaches_conformance_unjudged: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "unbound_generic_reaches_conformance_unjudged" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a call's generic parameter is never bound, so the call's result type stays an unresolved type parameter, and that unresolved type reaches a declared-type position (a direct-call argument, a declared return) where v1.compiler.infer's conformance does not judge it. The program is Accepted, and the value that arrives has a type the declaration forbids.",

    "SPECIMENS, executed 2026-10-05 with gunbc built at main ed68b5573b (gunbc run, fixture modules outside the corpus), each with take_s(s: String): (p8) `fn idf<R>(f: fn() -> R) -> R { f() }`; `take_s(s: idf(f: fn() { 5 }))` is Accepted and returns Int 5. (p8b) `fn app<T, R>(x: T, f: fn(T) -> R) -> R { f(x) }`; `take_s(s: app(x: 1, f: fn(v) { v }))` is Accepted and returns 1. (p7b) `fn apply2<T, R>(f: fn(T) -> R, x: T) -> R`; `take_s(s: apply2(f: fn(v) { v }, x: 1))` is Accepted and returns 1. In all three, R is fixed only by a lambda's return, which never binds it.",

    "DECLARED-RETURN SPECIMEN, same run: (p9) `fn into_string() -> String { idf(f: fn() { 5 }) }` is Accepted and returns Int 5, so the class is not confined to direct-call argument positions.",

    "DIAGNOSTIC CENSUS, measured with `gunbc compile --entry <fixture> --target dag` at main ed68b5573b, reading every diagnostic. At the position where the wrong value lands (take_s's `s: String`, or p9's declared return) NO specimen receives any diagnostic: p8 and p9 emit only the unrelated effect-summary advisory for a call through a function value; p8b and p7b emit DeclaredTypeInhabitanceUndecided (UndecidableGenericFormal) at the generic formal `x` of app/apply2, which is a different argument; and the control p7c, which is correct code, emits that identical advisory. Shape (2) `take_s(s: head_of(xs: [1], d: 2))` likewise draws advisories only at the list element and at `d`. So the advisory these specimens carry does not track the defect, and at the violated position the acceptance is silent. Contrast the sibling class generic_formal_unjudged_against_a_sibling_bound_argument, whose advisory fires exactly at the conflicting argument (`pick(a: 'x', b: 2)` draws it at `b`), which is why that class is mitigatable and this one is not.",

    "RELATION TO THE DECLARED DROP gunbc.rung_drop.direct_call_unbound_carried_generic_compat. That drop states that a direct-call argument whose PRODUCED VALUE still carries an unbound generic draws the UndecidableGenericFormal advisory. Measured above, it does not: the produced value `idf(..)`, typed as the unbound R, reaches take_s's `s` with no diagnostic. The drop's population is therefore narrower than it states (its formal-side arm is real and counted; its produced-value arm is silent), and the same change corrects its declaration. The two triggers are not one wall: the drop's restoration trigger substitutes every generic a call HAS BOUND before compatibility, which cannot fire for this class because here the parameter is bound by nothing. This row's trigger, refusing an unresolved generic at a declared-type position, is the wall for the never-bound remainder, and it is the one that would make the drop's produced-value arm loud instead of silent.",

    "MORE MEMBERS: gunbc.recurring_failure_mode generic_identity_decided_by_spelling shape (2), `take_s(s: head_of(xs: [1], d: 2))` Accepted with Int 1, is this class: its T is left unbound by the self-binding guard and then passes conformance unjudged. Its shape (1) srv3 refusal is the same unbound T meeting a method call, which does refuse. The neighbouring arm, where an unsolved variable is read back as a concrete type (Unit) instead of passing unjudged, is filed separately as unsolved_placeholder_reads_back_as_unit in gunbc#13354, NOT YET ON MAIN: until that lands, the name resolves only on that branch. Each new way to leave a generic unbound is a new admission route for this class, so it is the wall, not any one binding repair, that closes the class.",

    "HARM: silent wrongness (DESIGN section 5). The checker reports success for a program whose value contradicts a declared type, and nothing downstream refuses until the value is consumed at runtime, if then.",

    "NOT FIXED BY MORE BINDING: lifting particular routes (a lambda's return feeding a parameter, the #9416 narrowing in v1.compiler.infer above expr_contains_lambda) shrinks the population but leaves it open, because some parameters are genuinely not inferable from a call site. A round-2 MISMATCH is already judged on main: `ap<T, R>(f: fn(T) -> R, x: T, r: R)` with `ap(f: fn(v) { v }, x: 1, r: 's')` refuses `expected Callable(Int->String), got Callable(Int->Int)`. The gap is the UNSOLVED case, not the disagreeing one.",

    "PARTIAL CLOSURE, gunbc#13418: the LAMBDA-RETURN member closes. A generic fixed only by a lambda argument's return is now solved from it (v1.compiler.infer infer_call_arguments_generic_pass, lambda_callable_type and unify_callable_generics), so p8, p8b and p9 REFUSE `expected Primitive(String), got Primitive(Int)` at the located position instead of being Accepted. p7b STAYS OPEN: its lambda comes BEFORE the argument that fixes T, so in round 1 the lambda's return is still the unresolved callable_param and is dropped, round 2 contributes nothing, and R stays unbound and passes conformance unjudged. That is the stated two-round bound, and this row's wall (refuse an unresolved generic at a declared-type position) is what closes it, not more rounds.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: below the floor (silent acceptance). CEILING: 3 -- an unresolved type parameter at a declared-type position is a modeled state, decidable at the conformance site. NEXT TRIGGER: a call whose result type is an unresolved generic parameter, reaching a declared-type position, refuses with a typed located 'cannot infer <param> here; annotate' diagnostic. Evidence: p8, p8b and p7b refuse with that cause; `apply2(f: fn(v) { v }, x: 1)` under a declared -> Int is the accepted control. That wall refuses some code main accepts today, by design, and lands as its own step with its own census of the sites that newly refuse.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
